\begin{proof}
A reducible cubic over a field has a linear factor, hence a root in $\mathbb{F}_{31}$. If $x^3-9=0$, then $x^3=9$. By Fermat's criterion, every cube $y^3$ in $\mathbb{F}_{31}^{\times}$ satisfies $(y^3)^{10}=1$, since $y^{30}=1$. However,
\[
9^{10}=5\not\equiv 1\pmod{31}
\]
(as $9^2\equiv19$, $9^4\equiv20$, and $9^{10}\equiv20\cdot25\equiv5$). Thus $9$ is not a cube in $\mathbb{F}_{31}$, so $x^3-9$ has no root. Therefore it is irreducible in $\mathbb{F}_{31}$.
\end{proof}
